# RFC 076 exposed why this file needs to exist: a `.json` `file_path`
# response is now served exactly as written, byte for byte, no
# parse/reserialise round-trip. Before that fix, line endings inside a
# JSON fixture were invisible — every request re-parsed and
# re-serialised the content, so a checkout's own line-ending choice
# never survived to a response. Now it does: a fixture checked out with
# CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux/macOS produces two different response
# bodies from the same source file — not a server bug (it faithfully
# serves whatever is actually on disk), but a real, observed CI failure
# (three tests failed on `test (windows-latest)` only) the moment a
# byte-exact assertion met a platform-dependent checkout.
#
# `eol=lf` pins normal-text files to LF on checkout, on every platform,
# so the bytes a test asserts on are the bytes every CI runner actually
# has - not whatever `core.autocrlf` happened to be set to.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, not left to text=auto's content-sniffing heuristic — these
# extensions are exercised by apimock-server's own binary-content-type
# tests and dyn-route fixtures, and get this wrong (silently corrupted
# on checkout) rather than loud if a heuristic ever misjudges one.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.bmp binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.flac binary
*.ogg binary
*.m4a binary
*.aac binary
*.weba binary
*.mp4 binary
*.m4v binary
*.mpeg binary
*.mpg binary
*.avi binary
*.mov binary
*.webm binary
*.ogv binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
